<Article slug="after-hours-problem-hvac">
  <p>
    Here is the uncomfortable shape of a home services business in 2026: between 35% and 45% of your call volume arrives
    outside business hours, and the pickup rate on those calls is under 18%. That is not a small leak at the edges. It
    is close to half the demand, mostly unanswered.
  </p>

  <h2>The three numbers that define the problem</h2>
  <p>
    <b>Contractors miss 27% to 60% of inbound calls</b> depending on which study you read and how busy the season is.
    Even at the low end, a shop taking 50 calls a week is sending 13 people to voicemail.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Caller patience is short and getting shorter.</b> Roughly 30% of callers hang up within the first 60 seconds of
    being put on hold, and more than half are gone by 90 seconds. Figures vary between studies depending on how each one
    defines abandonment, so treat them as direction rather than precision. Every dataset points the same way.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>85% of people who reach voicemail never call back.</b> They call the next result. Your marketing paid for that
    lead and a competitor closed it.
  </p>

  <h2>What one missed call is worth</h2>
  <p>The per-trade numbers are stark:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>HVAC: $1,200 to $3,500 per missed call</li>
    <li>Plumbing: $800 to $2,000</li>
    <li>Electrical: $600 to $1,800</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Annualised, HVAC contractors lose an average of $45,600 a year to missed calls, and roughly $81,900 a year from
    missed after-hours emergency calls alone. Plumbers lose up to $125,000. These are averages across shop sizes, so
    treat them as direction rather than gospel, but the direction is unambiguous.
  </p>

  <h2>Why voicemail stopped working</h2>
  <p>
    Voicemail was fine when the alternative was also voicemail. It stopped working when a meaningful share of your
    competitors started answering at 9pm. A customer with a failing furnace in February is not leaving a considered
    message and waiting; they are working down a list until someone picks up. The first shop to answer books the job,
    and often the relationship.
  </p>
  <p>
    The traditional fix, a human answering service, has its own problem: they take a message. They do not know your
    service area, your job durations, your on-call rotation, or whether the caller is an existing customer with a
    system you installed two years ago. So you still wake up to a list of callbacks.
  </p>

  <h2>What a realistic fix looks like</h2>
  <p>Whatever you buy, it needs to do four things, in this order of importance:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <b>Recognise a genuine emergency and page a human immediately.</b> No heat with an elderly customer, gas smell,
      burst pipe, electrical burning. These cannot wait for a morning summary, and any system that treats them as
      routine is worse than voicemail.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Book real jobs into real slots.</b> Checking actual calendar availability, respecting your service area, using
      your job-type durations. A booking that you have to rearrange in the morning is not a booking.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Capture quote requests properly.</b> Job details, photos where possible, enough for you to price it without
      calling back to ask basics.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Never invent a price.</b> Quoting from a published price book is fine. Improvising is how you end up honouring
      a number you would never have given.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Vendors in this category publish large improvements in missed-call rates and dispatch speed after shops switch from
    a traditional answering service. We are not going to quote a specific number, because every figure we could find
    traces back to a company selling the software, and we have found no independent study of AI phone agents in the
    trades. Dispatch speed matters as much as the answer rate, because it is the difference between a same-day job and a
    next-week job, but you should measure it on your own shop rather than trusting anyone's marketing page, including
    ours.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <b>The arithmetic for a two-truck shop:</b> 60 calls a week, 40% missed, 24 unanswered. If 14 are bookable and you
      recover 8 to 10 a week at a $450 average ticket, that is $3,600 to $4,500 a week, or roughly $180,000 a year at
      the top end. Against a service costing $15,000 a year, you do not need the estimate to be accurate. You need it to
      be within an order of magnitude.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Check your own number first</h2>
  <p>
    Before believing any of this, pull your call log for the last 30 days. Most VoIP systems export answered versus
    missed, with timestamps. Look specifically at calls between 5pm and 8am and on weekends. Multiply the missed count
    by your own average ticket and your own booking rate. You now have your actual number, which is worth more than any
    benchmark in this article.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/home-services#calc">home services calculator</a> will do the arithmetic if you would rather not.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources, and a caveat about them</h2>
  <p>
    Worth stating plainly: the home services trade has no equivalent of Clio's Legal Trends Report. Nearly every
    statistic circulating about missed calls in this industry originates from a company selling answering software,
    including some of the numbers below, and those sources frequently cite each other rather than any primary data.
    We use them because they are the best available, we have removed the ones we could not trace at all, and we would
    rather show you the seams than pretend to a precision nobody in this market actually has.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://ainora.lt/blog/hvac-service-call-statistics-2026" rel="nofollow noopener">HVAC, Plumbing and Electrical Service Call Statistics, 2026</a> for missed-call rates, after-hours share, pickup rate and caller patience.</li>
    <li><a href="https://aioperations.io/missed-calls-cost-home-service-businesses-2026/" rel="nofollow noopener">What Missed Calls Actually Cost Home Service Businesses, 2026</a> for per-trade and annual loss figures.</li>
    <li><a href="https://calljolt.com/blog/hvac/home-service-industry-phone-statistics" rel="nofollow noopener">Home Service Phone Statistics, CallJolt, 2026</a> for missed-call reduction and dispatch speed after switching.</li>
    <li><a href="https://answeringagent.com/blog/missed-calls-business-revenue" rel="nofollow noopener">Missed Call Revenue Statistics, 2026</a> for callback behaviour.</li>
  </ul>
